//Arrays Sort method with Custom Comparator.
public class CustomComparatorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Product[] products = {
            new Product("Laptop", 1200.00),
            new Product("Smartphone", 800.00),
            new Product("Headphones", 150.00),
            new Product("Tablet", 800.00)
        };

        // Custom Comparator: Sort by price (descending), then by name (ascending)
        Comparator<Product> productComparator = Comparator
                .comparingDouble(Product::price).reversed()
                .thenComparing(Product::name);

        // Apply the custom comparator to the array
        Arrays.sort(products, productComparator);

        // Output the sorted results
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(products));
    }
}
//Variations - Alternative Custom Sorting Syntaxes

**Depending on your use case, you can implement custom Comparator logic using three different syntax variations:**

Style	Syntax Example	Best Used For
Method Reference / Chained	Comparator.comparingDouble(Product::price)	Standard, clean sorting based on existing fields or records.
Lambda Expression	(p1, p2) -> Double.compare(p2.price(), p1.price())	Inline logic that does not naturally fit a simple getter or extractor.
Anonymous Inner Class	new Comparator<Product>() { ... }	Legacy codebases or complex, multi-line nested routing comparisons.



